/-!
# Limit preservation on objects for the degree-`d` finite Hilbert stack

Let `F : 𝒳 ⟶ 𝒴` be a 1-morphism of stacks in groupoids over the big fppf site of a scheme `S`
which is representable by algebraic spaces and locally of finite presentation, and let `d` be a
natural number. Stacks 06CH asserts that the 1-morphism `p : ℋ_d(𝒳/𝒴) ⟶ 𝒴` of Stacks 05WD
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.forgetTarget`) is limit preserving on objects.

Unwinding the definition of limit preservation on objects
(`AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfOver.IsLimitPreservingOnObjects`, Stacks 06CU) for `p`, the assertion
is the following. Let `U = lim U_i` be an affine object of the site written as a cofiltered limit
of affine objects, let `y_i` be an object of `𝒴` over some `U_i`, and let
`Ξ = (U, Z, y, x, α)` be an object of `ℋ_d(𝒳/𝒴)` over `U` whose `y` is the restriction of `y_i`.
One has to produce `i' ≥ i` and an object `Ξ_{i'} = (U_{i'}, Z_{i'}, y_{i'}, x_{i'}, α_{i'})`
over `U_{i'}` restricting to `Ξ` and whose `y_{i'}` is the restriction of `y_i`.

The proof follows the source. The cover `Z ⟶ U` is of finite presentation (Stacks 02KB), so it
is the base change of a scheme of finite presentation over some `U_{i₁}` (Stacks 01ZM, part (1)),
which is finite locally free of degree `d` over some later `U_{i₂}` (the degree-`d` form of
Stacks 06AC); its base change `Z_{i'}` to a common refinement of `i` and `i₂` is finite over an
affine object of the site, hence again an affine object of the site (Stacks 000R (5)). The base
changes `U_k ×_{U_{i'}} Z_{i'}` along the later stages form a cofiltered system of affine objects
of the site with limit `Z`. The object `x` and the identification `α` composed with the
comparison `y ⟶ y_i` spread out along that system because `F` is limit preserving on objects,
which is Stacks 06CX: this is the source's appeal to Stacks 02ZX and Stacks 04AK for the
algebraic space representing `(Sch/U_i)_{fppf} ×_{y_i, 𝒴, F} 𝒳`, packaged once and for all.
Finally `y_{i'}` is a lift of `y_i` along `U_{i'} ⟶ U_i`, and the identification `α_{i'}` and the
morphism `Ξ ⟶ Ξ_{i'}` are obtained from the universal property of that lift.

The affine limits are indexed, as in the source, by a set: the index category of
`AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfOver.IsLimitPreservingOnObjects` is taken in the universe of the
schemes. For a larger index category the statement fails: for a field `K` which is not `u`-small,
the subrings generated by the finite subsets of `K` form a system indexed by `(Finset K)ᵒᵖ` whose
limit in schemes is the empty scheme, and for `𝒳` the empty algebraic space over `𝒴 = S` and
`d ≥ 1` there is an object of `ℋ_d(𝒳/𝒴)` over the empty limit but none over any stage.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.finiteHilbertCFG`: `ℋ_d(𝒳/𝒴)` as an object of the 2-category of
  categories fibred in groupoids over the big fppf site.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.finiteHilbertToTargetCFG`: the 1-morphism `ℋ_d(𝒳/𝒴) ⟶ 𝒴` of that
  2-category underlying `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.forgetTarget`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLimitPreservingOnObjects_finiteHilbertToTargetCFG`: **Stacks 06CH**.

## Implementation notes

The hypothesis that `F` is locally of finite presentation is the relative property of
Stacks 03YK written out longhand, exactly as in Stacks 06CX
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLimitPreservingOnObjects_iff_isLocallyOfFinitePresentation`), so that
no fppf-locality or base-change instance for local finite presentation of morphisms of algebraic
spaces is needed to state it.

An object of the fibre of `ℋ_d(𝒳/𝒴)` over `U` is a quintuple whose base object is only *equal*
to `U`, so the cover of such an object is a morphism `Z ⟶ P.U` rather than `Z ⟶ U`; the
transport along that equality is the `eqToHom` appearing in the proof, and it is harmless for
the degree condition by `Stacks.Morphisms.isFiniteLocallyFreeOfRank_postcomp`.

The system of base changes of the spread-out cover is built so that its image in schemes is
literally `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.baseChangeDiagram`, whose limit is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.isLimitBaseChangeCone`; its vertex is identified with `Z` by the
cartesian square of the spread-out cover.

The universes of the stacks are those of
`AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfOver.isLimitPreservingOnObjects_baseChange`: a stack whose morphisms
live in `max u v` and whose objects live in `max (u + 1) v w` has a finite Hilbert category with
the same two universes, which is what lets both appear as objects of one 2-category of categories
fibred in groupoids.

